body:

TitleFlow turns on-screen graphics for streams and recordings into plain files.
A pack is a folder of elements, and each element is an `element.html`, an
`element.css`, and an optional `element.js`. The files on disk are the only
source of truth, so there is no project database or hidden configuration, and
packs can be edited with any tool or generated from a CodePen-style design.

One small Go program serves three local surfaces. The runtime is the page an OBS
browser source loads. The editor offers live code panels with a preview that
uses the same composition logic as the runtime. The dock is a control surface for
operators, with editable text bindings, controls derived from `--tf-ui-*` CSS
variables, and named fire events such as Show and Hide. Changes reach the
overlay over server-sent events, so the stream does not need a reload.

The pack format is documented as a specification that works for both human
authors and AI converters, and a bundled skill helps build packs. Binaries for
Windows, Linux, and macOS (Intel and Apple Silicon) come from a single stateless
build with no runtime dependencies beyond the Go standard library and one
HTML-parsing module.

## Where it stands

This repository publishes the v0.4 line. A visual editing mode and other
extensions are being developed separately and are not part of it yet.
